TAM-EDIT

tam-edit is a personal fork of micro (https://github.com/micro-editor/micro),
the terminal text editor, kept by Fred at greenermoose/tam-edit
(https://github.com/greenermoose/tam-edit). It started as a rename of micro
and is being rewritten in C11 so that the editor links only libtam
(https://github.com/greenermoose/libtam) and the C library.

The fork point is upstream micro at commit ab37da30, 94 commits past the
v2.0.15 release, so master is micro 2.0.15 lineage. Upstream is tracked
through an upstream remote, and the Go module path stays
github.com/micro-editor/micro/v2 so upstream merges remain clean.

Report tam-edit bugs at greenermoose/tam-edit/issues
(https://github.com/greenermoose/tam-edit/issues), not upstream. The
Upstream section below maps micro's source and documentation to the
features being ported.


BRANCHES AND STATUS

  Branch   Tree                           Build                    Status
  ------   ----                           -----                    ------
  master   Go, micro's packages with      make build (Go 1.19+)    Behaviour
           the fork's changes                                      reference,
                                                                   archived
  c11      src/, a native C11 editor      make (libtam 0.7.0+)     1.0.3:
                                                                   parity gate,
                                                                   stabilization,
                                                                   packaging

New work happens on c11. ROADMAP.md holds the phase plan and the milestone
definitions. The Go tree has been pruned from c11 at 1.0.0 and remains on
master as the behaviour reference.

Version 1.0.3 (Milestone 5) completes the Phase 7 parity gate: full parity
audit (docs/PARITY_AUDIT.md), 57 ported VSCode/micro buffer corpus tests,
large-file/long-line/many-cursor/many-split stress tests, ASan+UBSan fuzz
harnesses (make fuzz), Section 8 performance verification, man page
(docs/tam-edit.1) and desktop entry (data/tam-edit.desktop), and libc-only
linkage.


VERSIONING

tam-edit follows semantic versioning, and while it is below 1.0.0 the
minor number names a milestone: 0.1.0 is the first daily-usable editor,
0.2.0 is views and configuration, and 1.0.0 is reserved for the parity gate
with micro. VERSION at the repository root is the only source (read by the Makefile); tam-edit --version
prints it next to the libtam version. The milestone table and the bump
rules are in ROADMAP.md (Milestones and Versions).


WHAT THE FORK CHANGES FROM MICRO

- The binary and command name are tam-edit.
- In the Go reference, configuration comes from TAM_EDIT_CONFIG_HOME, then
  $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tam-edit, then ~/.config/tam-edit. It never falls back
  to an existing micro configuration directory.
- The Go reference accepts TAM_EDIT_TRUECOLOR alongside MICRO_TRUECOLOR,
  which still works because it describes the terminal rather than the
  configuration.
- Go-reference Lua plugins may import tam-edit/* as well as micro/*.
- The reference toolchain is pinned in mise.toml; Go is optional for C11
  work.
- The C11 editor on c11 is C only and leaves out Go, tcell, Lua plugins,
  the plugin manager and the embedded terminal, which are v1 non-goals in
  ROADMAP.md.


BUILDING

The C11 editor (c11):

Requires a C11 compiler, make, pkg-config and libtam 0.7.0 or later.

  make                 # build/tam-edit
  make test            # unit tests
  make check-deps      # fail if anything other than libc is linked
  make install PREFIX=$HOME/.local/dev
  make uninstall

Run it as tam-edit FILE. PREFIX defaults to $HOME/.local/dev and the
install targets respect DESTDIR. If libtam is outside pkg-config's search
path, add its pkgconfig directory to PKG_CONFIG_PATH.

The Go editor (master):

Requires Go 1.19 or later.

  make build           # ./tam-edit
  make test

The Go tree has been pruned from c11 at 1.0.0; master retains the original
Go source and tests for reference. Syntax definitions, colorschemes, and
help pages remain in runtime/ alongside the embedded tables in data/.


LICENSE

The C11 editor uses GPLv3, as recorded in ROADMAP.md, with the full text in
LICENSE. libtam (https://github.com/greenermoose/libtam) also uses GPLv3.
micro's MIT permission notice is kept in LICENSE-MIT and covers the Go tree
and the runtime data that come from micro; LICENSE-THIRD-PARTY carries the
notices for the third-party libraries micro uses.


UPSTREAM

micro's own documentation -- every option, command, keybinding and plugin --
is at https://micro-editor.github.io and in micro-editor/micro
(https://github.com/micro-editor/micro). The Go tree on master carries the
same source, so "git show master:runtime/help/options.md" answers most
questions without leaving the clone.

The parts of the Go tree worth reading before porting a feature:

  Buffer, cursors, undo, save, search:
    internal/buffer/ -- buffer.go, cursor.go, eventhandler.go, save.go,
    search.go
  Commands, keybindings, panes, tabs:
    internal/action/ -- actions.go, bindings.go, command.go, pane.go
  Settings, colorschemes, runtime files:
    internal/config/ -- settings.go, colorscheme.go, rtfiles.go
  Drawing, statusline, soft wrap:
    internal/display/
  Highlighting state machine:
    pkg/highlight/ -- highlighter.go, parser.go
  Lua plugin API (deferred):
    internal/lua/
  Syntax definitions and colours:
    runtime/syntax/, runtime/colorschemes/

Bug reports and fixes come from micro's issue tracker
(https://github.com/micro-editor/micro/issues). master keeps upstream's
history, so git log there records what was fixed and why.
